{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    n = len(string)\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Check if the suffix starting from index i is a palindrome\n        if is_palindrome(string[i:]):\n            # If it is, the prefix string[:i] needs to be reversed and appended\n            prefix_to_reverse = string[:i]\n            return string + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n    \n    # This case should ideally not be reached if string is not empty,\n    # as at least string[n-1:] (the last character) is always a palindrome.\n    # However, for completeness or if the loop condition might not cover it,\n    # we can append the reverse of the whole string minus its longest palindromic suffix.\n    # The loop should always find a palindrome for i=n-1 (single character) or i=n (empty string if input is empty)\n    # The condition `is_palindrome(string[n:])` would be `is_palindrome(\"\")` which is True.\n    # So if no suffix from `string[0:]` to `string[n-1:]` is a palindrome, then `string[n:]` (empty string) is considered the palindrome.\n    # This means the entire `string[:n]` needs to be reversed and appended.\n    # The loop will find `is_palindrome(string[n-1:])` as true if the last char exists.\n    # The current loop correctly finds the longest such suffix and appends the prefix.\n    # The `return` inside the loop ensures we exit as soon as the first (and thus longest) palindromic suffix is found.\n    # This line should technically be unreachable if string is not empty, as `is_palindrome(string[len(string)-1:])` is always true.\n    # If the string itself is not a palindrome and no suffix is a palindrome (which is impossible as single chars are palindromes)\n    # the problem implies we should generate one by reversing the entire string and prepending it, but that's not exactly what the algorithm describes.\n    # The algorithm is: find longest suffix `S`, take `P` such that `string = P + S`, return `string + P_reversed`.\n    # The loop naturally finds the *first* `i` (from 0 to n-1) for which `string[i:]` is a palindrome.\n    # This `i` gives `string[:i]` as the prefix to reverse.\n    # If string is 'abc',\n    # i=0, 'abc' not pal\n    # i=1, 'bc' not pal\n    # i=2, 'c' is pal. prefix is 'ab'. return 'abc' + 'ba' = 'abcba'\n    # This is correct. The loop will always find a match."}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "def string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input are two strings a and b consisting only of 1s and 0s.\n    Perform binary XOR on these inputs and return result also as a string.\n    >>> string_xor('010', '110')\n    '100'\n    \"\"\"\n    len_a = len(a)\n    len_b = len(b)\n    max_len = max(len_a, len_b)\n\n    # Pad shorter string(s) with '0' on the left to match the maximum length\n    padded_a = a.rjust(max_len, '0')\n    padded_b = b.rjust(max_len, '0')\n\n    result_chars = []\n    for char_a, char_b in zip(padded_a, padded_b):\n        # XOR operation: if characters are different, result is '1', else '0'\n        if char_a != char_b:\n            result_chars.append('1')\n        else:\n            result_chars.append('0')\n\n    return ''.join(result_chars)"}
