{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "def find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    DEFAULT_TOLERANCE = 1e-12\n    DEFAULT_MAX_ITER = 1000\n    MAX_BRACKET_EXPANSION = 64\n\n    def _sign(x_val: float) -> int:\n        if x_val > 0:\n            return 1\n        elif x_val < 0:\n            return -1\n        return 0\n\n    def _bracket_root(coeffs: list, max_expand: int):\n        a = -1.0\n        b = 1.0\n        for _ in range(max_expand):\n            fa = poly(coeffs, a)\n            fb = poly(coeffs, b)\n\n            if _sign(fa) != _sign(fb):\n                return a, b\n            \n            # If a root is exactly on a boundary, consider it a valid bracket.\n            if fa == 0.0:\n                return a, a\n            if fb == 0.0:\n                return b, b\n\n            a *= 2.0\n            b *= 2.0\n        # Given the problem statement guarantees a solution for valid inputs,\n        # this point should not be reached with a sufficiently large max_expand.\n        # However, to avoid an implicit error (no return), we must return something.\n        # Returning the last expanded interval is a fallback.\n        return a/2.0, b/2.0 \n\n    def _bisection(coeffs: list, a: float, b: float, tol: float, max_iter: int):\n        fa = poly(coeffs, a)\n        fb = poly(coeffs, b)\n\n        if fa == 0.0: return a\n        if fb == 0.0: return b\n\n        for _ in range(max_iter):\n            mid = (a + b) / 2.0\n            fm = poly(coeffs, mid)\n\n            if abs(fm) <= tol or (b - a) / 2.0 <= tol:\n                return mid\n\n            if _sign(fa) != _sign(fm):\n                b, fb = mid, fm\n            else:\n                a, fa = mid, fm\n        \n        return (a + b) / 2.0\n\n    bracket_a, bracket_b = _bracket_root(xs, MAX_BRACKET_EXPANSION)\n    root = _bisection(xs, bracket_a, bracket_b, DEFAULT_TOLERANCE, DEFAULT_MAX_ITER)\n    return root"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    out = list(l)  # Create a shallow copy of the input list\n    \n    # Collect indices divisible by 3 and their corresponding values\n    third_indices = [i for i in range(len(l)) if i % 3 == 0]\n    third_values = [l[i] for i in third_indices]\n    \n    # Sort the collected values\n    sorted_third_values = sorted(third_values)\n    \n    # Place the sorted values back into the copied list at their original positions\n    for i, val in zip(third_indices, sorted_third_values):\n        out[i] = val\n        \n    return out"}
